blipster8 wrote:

> Dragontamer wrote:
>> After getting food poisoning from a yellow mold tin from my uncursed
>> Tinning kit, I think I misundersand something about this.

You can't get *food* poisoning from tins. Tinned food is never tainted. It
can be rotten, making you vomit and confusing you, but it doesn't kill
you (unless it's cockatrice meat or something intrinsically harmful like
that).

>> So what are the real rules on tinning/ tinning kits?

Tainted or poisonous meat becomes edible; you can tin giant zombies to eat
for strength, or killer bees to eat for poison resistance, without
getting food poisoning or poison damage.

Food in uncursed tins may be rotten (cause confusion, stunning, vomiting).
Cursed tins are always rotten. I'm not sure about blessed tins. 

Tin openers are useless; if you have time to wield it and open the tin,
you have time to open it without a tin opener. A blessed tin opens in one
turn anyway.

> AFAIK, eating a tin of something is exactly the same as eating the real
> corpse as far as special effects go, except that you get much less
> nutrition.

As far as special effects go, yes; leprechaun meat can still give you
teleportitis, dragon meat is certain to give you the dragon's intrinsic,
cockatrice meat will petrify you, yellow mold will make you hallucinate,
floating eye meat gives you telepathy.

